WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.924 --> 00:00:03.367
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:08.670
--energetic.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.110
So my name's Mike Smith.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.990
I work at Pfizer
in the UK, and I'm

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:16.179
very pleased to be here
to talk to you today.

00:00:16.179 --> 00:00:18.720
Because I realize that people
are lazy and easily distracted,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.803
I'm going to give you the
summary of the whole top

00:00:20.803 --> 00:00:21.720
right up front.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.100
I use parametrized rmarkdown
reports with child documents

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.500
to write up an exploratory
analysis, which

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.810
I shared with some
colleagues-- some of whom

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.837
were quantitative, some of
whom were not quantitative.

00:00:33.837 --> 00:00:35.670
The quantitative guys,
like the statistician

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.190
in the team and
clinical pharmacologist

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.090
from my department,
including my manager.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.860
There were other
nonquantitative guys

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.200
and, you know, it's maybe
unfair to call a clinician

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.140
nonquantitative,
but I'm going to.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.560
So this report was intended to
serve all of those purposes.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.460
Now, the analysis and the
stuff I'm going to show today

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.180
doesn't show that data because
of confidentiality reasons,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.000
but it has very
similar properties.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.860
But what I really want
to talk to you today

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.800
is about cutlery drawers
and what they say about you.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.880
Now, these cutlery
drawers are from people

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.680
that you might bump
into at this conference.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.300
And I think it's an interesting
exercise in how people arrange

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.900
things, structure things,
whether they do it

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.870
by size, whether they do
it by most frequently used,

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.880
the differences
between, perhaps, US

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.644
and European cutlery drawers.

00:01:28.644 --> 00:01:29.310
But here's mine.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.764
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:31.764 --> 00:01:33.930
If you visit my house,
you'll see my cutlery drawer.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.670
My wife will also
be very surprised

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.370
that you're visiting the house
and quite alarmed that you just

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:39.661
want to see the cutlery drawer.

00:01:39.661 --> 00:01:40.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.210
Now, I did the tidyverse
Train-the-Trainer course,

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.870
and thanks to Greg and
Garrett for teaching on that.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.950
And I am now taking
every opportunity

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.680
to have a learning
experience for the tidyverse.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.720
So if you take
cutlery drawer, you

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:55.962
might want to group by type.

00:01:55.962 --> 00:01:58.170
You want to gather those
things together and arrange.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.170
And if you could do that
from my cutlery drawer,

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.510
I would be really happy.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:05.190
New hashtag for the
conferences-- untidyverse.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:10.350
And I'm really
pleased that the XKCD

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:14.160
views the need for tidiness in
the house in very similar ways.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.627
Basically, if you've
got Wi-Fi and a laptop,

00:02:16.627 --> 00:02:18.210
you can put all your
other possessions

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.190
in a big bucket
marked miscellaneous.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:23.820
OK, here's the
premise for my talk.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.770
My brain is lazy, shallow,
and very easily distracted.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:32.460
I hesitate to say that
your brain is the same,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.310
but then we had Joe Chang
up here telling us all

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.760
that our intuition sucks.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.520
So I think some people
in this audience

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.490
must have a brain that's lazy,
shallow, and easily distracted.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.270
Now, we're all familiar
with this plot.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.410
It's from the R for
Data Science book,

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.270
and it gives us a
framework or a structure

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.700
for understanding any analysis.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.280
And on the Not So Standard
Deviations podcast recently,

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.780
there was a good
discussion about this,

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.200
and they came to the
conclusion that really this

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.150
is just a framework.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.450
It's a mental model.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.220
Real data analysis
don't necessarily

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.670
have to follow all of
these steps or in sequence.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.250
So let me explain to you
something about my data

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.950
analysis and how it
works in practice.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.560
And I'm sure that these
experiences I'm going to relate

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.170
are just for me alone,
so it's not you guys.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.690
You have your stuff
well together.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.140
This is just me.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:21.900
OK, here we go.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.350
So I go, and I get an email
from my colleague who has

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:26.670
the link to the data source.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.430
I download that data
source, I read it into R,

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.770
I wrangle the data,
and I plot it.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.080
And then there's some
time to step back,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.110
to reflect on my plot, to
think about what it looks

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.530
like so that after lunch, I
can come back and make better

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.580
plots.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.390
And I might fit a preliminary
model to the data.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:46.680
Hey, it's the end of the
day, it's time to go home.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.410
So the next day,
I come in, and I

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.300
find an email saying
that the team has

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.640
found an error in
the data, so here's

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.170
the new version of the data.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.300
And if your workflow
is not reproducible,

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.460
you're in a world of hurt here.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.790
So because I'm reproducible,
I go to my markdown.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:05.580
I change the input data,
I recompile my report,

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.230
I see what
differences there are.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.490
I check against the
previous version.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.730
I'm good to go on.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.070
I discuss the
findings with my boss.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:18.277
I circulate the report,
and my job is done.

00:04:18.277 --> 00:04:18.860
Wait a minute.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.850
Did anyone get
kind of distracted

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.820
by the text in light gray?

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.550
Perhaps it's not just me then.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.520
OK.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.610
The other thing is,
did anyone notice

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.810
that the transform
and visualize bits

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.690
from the R for Data Science
diagram were back to front

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.910
and swapped over?

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:39.510
Cognitive load
theory, score one.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.370
So six months pass.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.290
This is not an exaggeration.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:47.790
I got an email yesterday
from my manager saying,

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.082
you know that team and the
work that you did for them?

00:04:50.082 --> 00:04:51.540
They're interested
in your results.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.430
Can you dig them
out and share them?

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:56.160
Now, if you're anything
like me, what happens

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.130
is you pop open your R script
or something like that,

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.960
and you think, what
the heck did I do?

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.070
All right.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:10.620
But to the rescue, for me,
comes R Markdown and Notebooks.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.300
These guys are now
saving my life.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.680
I love Notebooks.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.419
Thank you, eBay and team who
are working on Notebooks.

00:05:17.419 --> 00:05:18.960
And whenever you're
writing, you need

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:20.876
to think about who is
the audience that you're

00:05:20.876 --> 00:05:21.450
writing for.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.430
Well, for me, when
I write a Notebook,

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.110
it's for distracted me.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.740
It's the one that is
hopping between activities,

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.710
not focusing 24/7 on my
nice little analysis,

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:34.110
but with a billion things to do.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.150
It's also the future
me-- the six months later

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.580
me that pops open the
Markdown document and goes,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.300
all right, I'm good,
because I've got my code,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.150
I've got the explanation,
I can see the outputs.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:49.080
I've even got text that
explains what I was thinking.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.420
Hurrah.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.000
But here's another
audience for your reports--

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.100
it could be a
quantitative colleague

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.710
who wants to see code,
who wants to see data, who

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:00.330
wants to see your
assumptions, who

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.950
wants to dig into your
residual plots from your model.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.470
I can guarantee you the
nonquantitative people

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.330
won't want to see that.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.660
So it's good if you can
try to balance up and try

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:14.070
to have techniques that
would allow you to hide

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.590
the bits that the
nonquantitative people want

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.260
to see, but also allow the
quantitative people to get what

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:20.385
they want from that report.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:26.830
So back to Notebooks
just for a second.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:28.800
I realized--
[INAUDIBLE] thank you--

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.300
that there is a debate here.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:34.210
But for analysis, if
you're writing analysis,

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:36.580
I think Notebooks are fantastic.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.810
And back to Garrett's
stuff at Markdown,

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:42.340
if you're writing more code--

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:45.250
sorry, writing more
comments than code,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.770
then you should be using
R Markdown in Notebooks.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:50.560
If you're using writing
more code than comments,

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.380
write more comments
and use R Markdown.

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:56.377
But if you're not
writing for analysis,

00:06:56.377 --> 00:06:58.210
then I really recommend
that you go and read

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:01.960
this blog post because
it is very interesting.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.900
Also, because I'm
lazy, I knew my manager

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:07.090
would come back and ask
for the same analysis

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:09.900
across the three endpoints
that are in my data.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:10.510
OK?

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:12.700
So I'm trying to
set up my report,

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:16.000
to answer quantitative,
nonquantitative, and for three

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.193
different endpoints.

00:07:18.193 --> 00:07:21.490
[AUDIO OUT]

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:23.920
--paste your code
more than three times,

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.020
what should you do?

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:27.135
Write a function.

00:07:27.135 --> 00:07:29.510
If you have to perform an
analysis across more than three

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:31.510
endpoints, what do you do?

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:35.944
Do you write multiple
R Markdown reports?

00:07:35.944 --> 00:07:37.610
Parameterized reports,
thanks very much.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:38.500
Otherwise, why
would you be here?

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:39.460
I mean, honestly.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:40.870
I'm not that entertaining.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:44.950
Anyway, back to XKCD.

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:48.849
XKCD talks about automation,
and the top panel talks

00:07:48.849 --> 00:07:50.140
about the theory of automation.

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:52.090
It's a bit like the
theory of data science.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:55.300
It's the mental model for
what we all hope will happen.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:57.490
We automate things,
we can get on them,

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:59.150
we can do a completely
different work.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.450
The reality is that when
you automate things and try

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:03.580
to be clever, often you
wind up troubleshooting

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.700
the thing that's gone wrong.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:09.370
But here's the thing that
makes all of this work

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:10.830
is the YAML header parameters.

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:14.136
Now, if you're familiar
with R Markdown,

00:08:14.136 --> 00:08:15.760
then you'll know
about the YAML header.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.640
If you're not familiar
with R Markdown,

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.670
this may be a bit of a
stretch, but bear with me.

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:24.130
So the YAML header
says something

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:26.800
about the document-- it says the
title, the author, the date it

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:30.880
was done, and it says
something about the formatting

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:31.570
for the output.

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:33.179
So here, it's an
HTML document that

00:08:33.179 --> 00:08:34.650
has some certain attributes.

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:38.380
The bit I've highlighted in red
is the bit we need to focus on.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.429
These are parameters that you
can pass into your document,

00:08:42.429 --> 00:08:46.780
and it comes into your
document like an R object

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:48.910
that you can then use,
you can compute on,

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.130
you can do all kinds of
clever things with it.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.820
So here in my
document, in my header,

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:58.080
I've got a parameter
which is called endpoint.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.720
It has a default
value of HAMDTL17.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.600
But it also has three
distinct choices,

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:06.640
so it's not just a free text.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:09.610
You have to choose one
of those three options.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:11.590
Also, I've got a
boolean parameter, which

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:14.139
is called quantitative
audience, and if that is true,

00:09:14.139 --> 00:09:16.180
then I'm going to include
a bunch of other stuff.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:18.850
But if it's false, then
this is a stripped back

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:21.660
report just for the
nonquantitative audience.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.470
OK?

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:25.900
Now, the specification
of those is

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:28.540
very close to what you
might do with Shiny inputs

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.000
if you're familiar with them.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.310
So then, when you're
ready, you can

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:34.880
choose to knit that document.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.400
And if you just hit
the Knit button,

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:37.790
you'll get the default values.

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:40.210
But if you can choose
to knit with parameters,

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:43.660
then a little pop-up box
comes up, a bit like Shiny.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.500
You choose your
endpoints, you determine

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.560
whether you're in the
quantitative audience,

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:49.610
and you can knit your document.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:52.411
Now, parameters are
really cool because you

00:09:52.411 --> 00:09:53.410
can do things with them.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:57.580
So here, I've embedded
the parameter endpoint,

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:01.750
or it's params$endpoint, and
that value can get pasted

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:03.040
into text.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.800
So then, you can talk about
the endpoint for the report

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:09.970
that you're talking about,
you can pass the parameter in

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:12.160
and use it within
the markdown text.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:17.170
You can use it in the header or
the axis labels for the ggplot.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.870
And so it can be used
in a variety of ways,

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:20.830
sky's the limit.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:22.970
OK.

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:24.800
But The other thing
that's kind of cool

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.440
is that you can
use the parameters

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:31.100
in determination of whether
the code chunk runs or not.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:35.430
So here I am saying if it's
not the quantitative audience--

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:38.120
in other words, if it's the
nonquantitative audience-- then

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:39.040
hide all the code.

00:10:42.639 --> 00:10:44.930
The other thing that makes
this work is in the top box,

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.304
and that's because
what I did was

00:10:46.304 --> 00:10:50.064
to rename all of the
endpoint columns in my data

00:10:50.064 --> 00:10:51.730
with the name of the
parameter endpoint.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:55.670
So that, then, downstream, I can
fit a linear model on something

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.040
called outcome and not
on something called

00:10:58.040 --> 00:10:59.901
params$endpoint.

00:10:59.901 --> 00:11:02.150
It just cleans up the code,
and it makes things easier

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:05.490
to see further down.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:10.010
You can choose to run
code chunks at all

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:12.450
based on parameters.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:15.200
The other thing that I've
used is a child document

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.810
because, if you remember,
when I'm presenting this

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:19.940
to the quantitative
audience, I want

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:22.130
to pull in some
extra information.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:24.200
The code chunks
will run, or not,

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.654
according to the
settings here, but you

00:11:26.654 --> 00:11:28.820
might want some additional
bit of text that comes in

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:30.960
and says, OK, for the
quantitative guys,

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:33.170
here's what I'm doing.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:35.450
And that's where the
child document comes in.

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.090
They're just plain
text, R Markdown files,

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:41.480
and those guys get
pulled in if needed.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:44.000
So for the quantitative
audience, what you see here

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.070
is that we're showing the code.

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:48.440
I've run the chunk,
you can see the data.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:51.020
And the bits at the bottom
that says Data Manipulations

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:52.610
is that child document text.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:57.490
Now, if you're--

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:01.030
Tareef kind of stole some of
my thunder in his opening talk,

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:01.720
but that's OK.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:04.510
He's the president, that's fine.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:06.210
What you can do
in RStudio Connect

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.230
is that you can go across
to the left-hand side

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:10.090
where it says Input.

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:12.790
You can pop that open, and
it will have the same ability

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:16.660
to select parameters
and define what

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.880
the report is going to run.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.520
But the other nice thing
is that if you do that,

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.800
and you save those
things as named objects,

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:25.300
then in RStudio
Connect, you've just

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:30.230
got this little drop-down
menu of precompiled reports.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:34.810
So what you might want to do
is to set up commonly used--

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.330
here, it was all right
because there's only

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:40.200
six reports in total
that could be done.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.850
But it's that ability that
if someone has already

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:45.400
run this report, you can
just quickly grab it,

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:46.870
and it doesn't
have to recompile.

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:52.520
So, more about parametrisation.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:56.890
So what we saw was when
we render the report,

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:59.060
you go to the render
with parameters or knit

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:00.320
with parameters.

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:03.620
But from the command line, you
can pass in your parameters

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.500
just through a list
like this, and it's

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:08.930
really straightforward.

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:10.679
The second thing
is you might want

00:13:10.679 --> 00:13:12.470
to change your analysis,
depending on which

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:13.594
endpoint you're looking at.

00:13:13.594 --> 00:13:16.160
If you're looking at
a categorical outcome,

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:19.010
you're not going to want fit
a linear model or at least you

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:21.140
shouldn't.

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:25.580
So in that case, you may want to
tailor your analysis, depending

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:26.690
on the parameter endpoint.

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:28.490
But again, it's
just another thing

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:31.610
that you can compute
on within a chunk.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:34.760
Also, if something goes
wrong with your analysis,

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:36.770
then if you're handling
the error appropriately,

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:38.810
if you're using tryCatch
or something like that,

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:42.500
you can compute on that and
then have a child document that

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.270
says something helpful
like, you know,

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:47.272
something's gone wrong, contact
your friendly data scientist.

00:13:47.272 --> 00:13:48.480
You know, here's his details.

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:55.390
So the other last
thing, back to XKCD,

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:58.520
is how long should you spend
parametrising your report

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:00.200
and setting things up?

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:02.730
Now, this is kind of alarming.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:07.070
I know that JD Long has also
talked to this graph today.

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:09.800
But the thing I want
to impress on you

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:14.290
is, basically, this
is over five years.

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:15.860
So if you spend
a day or two days

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:19.571
or five days sorting this
out and only two people

00:14:19.571 --> 00:14:21.320
are going to use it
once every six months,

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:22.966
maybe I wasted my time.

00:14:22.966 --> 00:14:25.340
But on the other hand, I got
a conference talk out of it,

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:26.402
so that's good.

00:14:26.402 --> 00:14:28.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:30.526
Anyway, thank you very
much for your attention,

00:14:30.526 --> 00:14:31.650
feel free to ask questions.

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:35.250
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.600
Thank you very much, Mike.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:39.850
We have time for questions
before the break for lunch.

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:40.585
Hands, please.

00:14:48.085 --> 00:14:51.120
OK, can I ask one myself?

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:54.990
So you're building all of
this wonderful machinery,

00:14:54.990 --> 00:14:56.610
and then you move
on and somebody else

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:59.580
has to maintain it, how many of
the people that you work with

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:03.360
understand and value the
machinery that you've just

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.340
shown us?

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.430
Oh, that's a tricky question.

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:11.280
Not many at present.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:12.530
And how are you tackling that?

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:14.279
Well, I've just done
the Train-the-Trainer

00:15:14.279 --> 00:15:15.880
on the tidyverse, and I'm here.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:18.990
So I'm going to go back and
evangelize and, you know.

00:15:18.990 --> 00:15:20.820
But yes, it's a
problem that we need

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:23.940
to try to roll this out and
get more and more people

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:25.140
familiar with how it works.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:29.390
